WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.120
Let us take an example.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.000
Here, we will look at the heights

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.500
of people in a human population.

4
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.400
Using the short piece of R
code on the right, we generate

5
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.260
a population of 10,000 people,

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.440
with a mean height of 170 cm
and a standard deviation of 7.

7
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.260
From this distribution of 10,000 people,

8
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.240
we randomly draw a sample of 40 people,

9
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.040
and calculate the population
mean. In this case,

10
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.660
we have the entire population,
so we can calculate its mean: 170 cm.

11
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.900
We also calculate the sample mean,
which varies from one sample to another.

12
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.660
In this graph, we compare

13
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.580
the mean of our original sample.
We can see all the values,

14
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.620
the heights of all 10,000
people in the population,

15
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.980
the population mean,
and, shown by the red dashed line,

16
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:14.420
our sample mean,
which will sometimes be higher

17
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.320
and sometimes lower than
the population mean.

18
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.560
With each draw, we observe a difference

19
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.100
above or below the sample mean.

20
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.820
The question is whether
we can use the sample to assess,

21
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.280
using a test, whether our population has

22
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.530
a mean height of 170 cm.

23
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:49.530
We test our sample against
the null hypothesis, H0,

24
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.090
that the mean
of the underlying population,

25
00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.370
and therefore of our sample,
is indeed 170 cm.

26
00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:02.050
If we reject it, we accept
the alternative hypothesis, H1:

27
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.350
that this mean differs from 171.

28
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.150
A test is always
constructed in the same way:

29
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:14.300
we choose

30
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.820
a null hypothesis, H0, and test it.

31
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.220
Depending on the risk of error
we are willing to accept,

32
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.080
we decide whether to reject
H0 and therefore accept H1.

33
00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.940
More specifically, in this example,

34
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.740
we use a t-test. From our sample,

35
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:44.220
we calculate t. This statistic

36
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:50.440
is defined so that, when our sample mean

37
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:56.280
approaches 170 cm,
the smaller the difference

38
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:02.450
between our sample mean
and the target value,

39
00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.890
the closer t gets to zero.
The larger the difference,

40
00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:12.410
the farther t moves from zero,
in either direction,

41
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:17.530
following the t distribution shown here.

42
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.700
The total area under this
curve of the t statistic

43
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.060
is equal to 1. The small areas shown here

44
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.120
in orange each correspond
to our chosen threshold, alpha,

45
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.980
divided by 2. We usually
set alpha to 0.05, or 5%,

46
00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.780
so here we have 0.025,

47
00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:48.260
or 2.5% of the area, on either side.

48
00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.700
When we calculate t from our sample,

49
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:58.220
if it is far from zero,
either positively or negatively,

50
00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.300
it falls within these orange regions,

51
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:08.440
and we decide to reject
our null hypothesis, H0,

52
00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.760
that the sample mean is 170 cm,

53
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.780
with a risk of being wrong below 5%.

54
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.640
That is the risk of error we accept

55
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.920
when rejecting H0
at a significance level below 5%.

56
00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.550
All tests are constructed this way.

57
00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:38.350
In the table at the bottom right,

58
00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.660
we decide either not to reject

59
00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.700
or to reject H0, which may
actually be true or false.

60
00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.740
Starting with the right-hand column,

61
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.710
rejecting H0 when it is false
is the correct decision.

62
00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:02.370
But if we reject it
when it is true, we make

63
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.370
a type I error, corresponding
to our chosen significance level, alpha.

64
00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.830
This does not tell us
the type II error rate:

65
00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.830
the error of failing
to reject H0 when it is false.

66
00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:20.690
That is calculated differently

67
00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:25.250
and cannot be derived
directly from our sample.
